  "response": "The choice between diffusion and independent invention is decided by the pattern of evidence rather than by the intrinsic plausibility of the trait itself. Diffusion explains a shared cultural trait as transmission from one society to another through contact, and it is this understanding that has made a continuous geographic distribution of the trait across the territory between two regions count as support for diffusion. Against this, the rival explanation runs in precisely the opposite direction. Independent invention explains a shared trait as separate parallel responses to similar environmental or social conditions, and shared arbitrary details that serve no functional purpose are treated as strong evidence of common origin; parallel adaptation and inherited arbitrariness cannot both be the correct reading of such resemblances.\n\nThat same evidential principle \u2014 the weight placed on arbitrary, functionless correspondences \u2014 shows why loanwords shared between the languages of two societies indicate direct contact between their speakers. Two further instruments do different kinds of work. Ancient DNA analysis can detect genomic admixture produced by contact between two populations; radiocarbon dating, by contrast, establishes the relative chronology of a trait's first appearance in each region. That chronological role cannot be squared with the fact that radiocarbon dating measures the decay of potassium-40 in organic tissue; the two accounts of the method cannot both be true.\n\nThe Polynesian sweet potato shows how a dispute of this kind is brought to a close. Sweet potato (Ipomoea batatas) is native to tropical America. The crop was domesticated in Island Southeast Asia, a homeland that rules out an American nativity, since the plant cannot have originated in both places. Charred sweet potato remains from Mangaia in the Cook Islands were radiocarbon dated to about AD 1000. Herbarium specimens collected during early European voyages have been used to reconstruct sweet potato lineages in Polynesia.\n\nSome nine centuries after those tubers were charred and buried, the question of transport across the Pacific was addressed by experiment rather than by excavation. Thor Heyerdahl's 1947 Kon-Tiki raft voyage travelled from Peru to the Tuamotu Islands. A demonstration that a voyage is feasible does not establish that the transfer actually occurred. A controversy of this type is therefore closed only by a find that ties the two societies to each other directly \u2014 a dated specimen, a genome, a borrowed word \u2014 and not by a demonstration of what was merely within reach.",
